@@ -2,6 +2,31 @@
 
 This project is a sample news application built with Astro, designed to serve as a testing ground for new features in Chrome DevTools.
 
+## Developer diagnostics
+
+The homepage includes independent browser-to-device and browser-to-CLI status checks.
+No connection request is made until the reader submits the corresponding form. Defaults
+are `http://192.168.1.100:8080/api/status` and `http://127.0.0.1:45678/health`;
+readers can edit the full URL, including port and path. These are example endpoints,
+not services bundled with this application.
+
+Serve the page over HTTPS. Each endpoint must support a read-only GET, return a 2xx
+status with an optional text/JSON body, and include `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`
+for the deployed page's origin (scheme, host, and port, without the `/devtools-times/`
+path). Requests omit cookies and authentication; redirects are rejected. Local
+network and loopback browser permissions are checked separately, with a
+user-initiated fallback when permission queries are unsupported. Browser support
+for HTTP local connections varies; use a current Chromium browser or an endpoint
+with trusted HTTPS if blocked. No application-server proxy is involved.
+
+LAN URLs accept private/link-local IPv4 addresses or `.local` names. CLI URLs accept
+the 127/8 range, `localhost`/`.localhost`, or `[::1]`. Each request has a 10-second
+timeout and a 4,096-character response preview. A successful status check is a snapshot, not
+a persistent telemetry session. Permission changes are observed while mounted.
+
+Run the address-space and request-policy tests with `node --test tests/diagnostics.test.mjs`
+(Node with TypeScript stripping support), and compile with `pnpm build`.
+
 
 ```sh
 pnpm install && pnpm run start
